-- title:
--   Theorem 2.1 — Kraus criterion for irreducibility
-- statement:
--   Let $\Phi:B(\mathbb C^d)\to B(\mathbb C^d)$ be completely positive, $d>0$, with a finite Kraus representation $\Phi(X)=\sum_jV_j^*XV_j$. Let $\mathcal A$ be the unital complex algebra generated by the $V_j$, without adding adjoints. Davies irreducibility means that $\Phi(P)\le cP$, for a projection $P$ and $c>0$, forces $P=0$ or $P=1$. Then
--
--   $$\Phi\text{ is irreducible}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\mathcal A\psi=\mathbb C^d\ \text{for every }\psi\ne0.$$
--
--   No normalization or trace-preservation condition is imposed. This converts irreducibility into cyclicity of the Kraus-generated algebra.
-- source:
--   Entropic fluctuations of quantum dynamical semigroups, arXiv:1305.4409v2, PDF p. 4, Theorem 2.1

